#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/difficulty 1.6353301804901486 :topic/id #uuid "dc19c9ce-6319-5943-8842-352e3c3735c7" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385}] :topic/math-academy-id 987 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050960}] :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558850 :question-generator/id #uuid "4b7826ff-ee39-5236-8e58-684d7427ea22" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186083245.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa64069-1206-5421-8a4f-a6e818653412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080166} {:answer/id #uuid "25e79155-ec81-5750-8024-8b85f305474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{12}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080167} {:answer/id #uuid "287cb0ff-c745-5fe1-847e-2f4eb5158055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{8}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080168} {:answer/id #uuid "52f88dfd-78ed-5cbe-9062-4303b028313d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{7}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080169} {:answer/id #uuid "571c9ee7-eb34-5dec-8694-3d455d4bf27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "571c9ee7-eb34-5dec-8694-3d455d4bf27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080170} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c73154-e807-5407-a20e-cfe049bc0a94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080165}] :question/id #uuid "8def6986-b021-50a6-91db-c2da4068ed38" :question/math-academy-id "q-15558" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d40bd1a54762b360dc590f014d1df9731168f8620df07ee8f0294bd078fc8da5.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{4} + t, y = t^{2}, - ∞ < t < ∞.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t =-1$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "At $t=-1$, the point is $(0,1)$, while $x'(t)=4t^3+1=-3$ and $y'(t)=2t=-2$. The tangent slope is $2/3$, so the normal slope is $-3/2$. The normal is $y=1-3x/2$; setting $y=0$ gives $x=2/3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5faf98-bf15-5a2b-9fdc-09a9a727c6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273199} {:answer/id #uuid "5f37ca8d-3ea5-5dc1-85e2-3a3270cb4ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273206} {:answer/id #uuid "7e745226-ecb3-5bd0-91a4-532e194cf6a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273471} {:answer/id #uuid "818af87a-0a7b-5074-95df-e9381f58f773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec910-ef36-5928-896c-acb859df3e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{2em}{y}^{'}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)}{{x}^{'}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f344a5d-f20b-5d29-bc4b-455872b79c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254087} {:answer/id #uuid "9e617510-b480-5d70-b155-ca6d7883d5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255062} {:answer/id #uuid "b77a05d6-9403-5a91-8f6f-1d9bf6e028b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255297} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa266e3-b7f3-5389-b0e2-d0701e510855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255519} {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523} :answer-field/id #uuid "38fc7afa-faeb-58be-bde2-8b46b42e1581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f7632b-4b69-541a-a152-af5eeab4cd2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122898" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c918410-e361-5bcd-9d06-8fb2ed574022" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d59423d-64dc-523d-9ab7-74063ac68307" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12a180e2-5235-5f82-b761-d705d49812b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350386" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093} :answer-field/id #uuid "429e4c9a-9cfb-50e6-a993-4807d39b6109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11efaf-e900-5155-8422-a40a0e3088ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe83c43c-ad52-5fa5-817f-abc0952cf819" :question/math-academy-id "q-350395" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} {:answer/id #uuid "8002ae58-b098-5bda-ad00-0c7449da285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334127} {:answer/id #uuid "88ce2a6a-ee62-5f62-9806-39eacf4d3091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334202} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd1a297-06d6-51de-b3b8-df8da09b3b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334652} {:answer/id #uuid "ea795a17-22c8-5438-a337-67f964713d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} :answer-field/id #uuid "48542b45-fcfe-5b1a-aede-206d84c3eeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dafa44c9-9831-51c8-95fc-4a16466f716d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6858" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6379c3-3e1c-5a08-ae54-d99f43489aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400067} {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9efa8-1c48-5867-a1e9-734559b5450d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400069} {:answer/id #uuid "f85a9aab-60c9-5ec0-8bc2-0588e38d3687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400070} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e29ac-c571-528d-b8c7-bb6ee10f19df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e41e50-ea1d-54b4-8438-62f4c2b9fa61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400066}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "138e1f42-3fb6-5013-87c6-eb59c3a912d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122931" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5eb255d-3cfa-5ae7-b5a0-ab9f9f6b8e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403824} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb81c4-43fa-5409-88e4-4f5ef499dd13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403825} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} {:answer/id #uuid "9555e468-7e78-5b3e-afb9-8e76c044cd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403827} {:answer/id #uuid "13841314-a76c-5620-906a-428ce704c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc1b61e-03fa-5261-ae12-a9f784057a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403823}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75288" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041f8bb1-395a-5761-a87c-1b044587e237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527315} {:answer/id #uuid "5577dcb2-91aa-5255-8e1f-f21bfb8168c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527470} {:answer/id #uuid "62850344-0899-50d9-b54b-f07542ecb296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527493} {:answer/id #uuid "a64f5e23-38b3-551e-9f55-fba8ae74a486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\pi ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527651} {:answer/id #uuid "ab311e64-5391-5036-a765-998bb36b2623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab311e64-5391-5036-a765-998bb36b2623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527659} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9630a8b-9a3c-56a1-882a-b2e2265a7e4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1cf055f3-7724-5017-8bd4-b9d8370b23a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122943" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{\\pi }\\right),\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\pi $ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi }\\right)=4\\pi (0)=0, \\\\ y & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{\\pi }\\right)+4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{\\pi }\\right)+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi }\\right)+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4\\cdot 0+4}=-\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,}=4\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-\\pi )$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-\\pi ) & =4(x-0) \\\\ y & =4x-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ is the $x$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9012e4-4080-5eed-b0f1-ea73de461ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d9012e4-4080-5eed-b0f1-ea73de461ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527374} :answer-field/id #uuid "23614fcf-fbe4-5be9-ae9e-34482a74c5f3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c25e94b-079d-51a9-bf98-4849a2d4378b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c25e94b-079d-51a9-bf98-4849a2d4378b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527369} :answer-field/id #uuid "298c6150-0d93-5585-9a25-200dda26c396" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527815}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8516a769-888c-5185-902e-1a5250ccaa04" :question/math-academy-id "q-350393" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0<t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\n$$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{3(3)}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "716c77ef-6905-55d3-a9a4-8d54e5510e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716c77ef-6905-55d3-a9a4-8d54e5510e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527520} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d17d6d-2bde-5720-8a1c-bab17264def6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "947f538f-26d4-542e-9645-e3e57363dc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-350384" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line at $C$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the Cartesian equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =x+2 \\\\ y & =x+2+1 \\\\ y & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the normal line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081ac015-24cb-5663-8688-ae1b7ff7348b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527322} {:answer/id #uuid "434c5620-db8a-5f85-858a-d1decc931c4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4{e}^{2}-2}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527437} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cc7b58-1e78-5215-ae55-4b924f699a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{\\,e\\,}x-\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527653} {:answer/id #uuid "b63fdc98-6ffe-5091-898f-611ee1b4b7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527679} {:answer/id #uuid "cd19d4c1-53f7-5df5-ae10-f2400625057c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}-4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63fdc98-6ffe-5091-898f-611ee1b4b7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527679} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac112813-4b74-55f0-8f43-22f46db91806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527881}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f508d724-e816-5421-91b6-b8baa491ba13" :question/math-academy-id "q-122928" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=4, \\\\ y & =2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=2e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(4,2e).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{4}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=e} & =\\frac{e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+e}{2}=e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2e)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2e & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\\right)(x-4) \\\\ y-2e & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8c6fb1-ed90-53ee-b5fd-e3890426b5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558814} {:answer/id #uuid "3834fd3e-bd83-5566-bbc4-0b00c26796cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- 2 x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558819} {:answer/id #uuid "60cb49ec-2f0a-5e7b-a841-b526a4e944b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558823} {:answer/id #uuid "d7377abd-9279-5a9c-8851-cce20d62767f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558841} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbb998b-9f2d-5ba4-8ced-8879b75abf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60cb49ec-2f0a-5e7b-a841-b526a4e944b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558823} :answer-field/id #uuid "d02207d8-8c71-523b-bb49-78268960f3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0938f21c-1f96-532e-9f2d-e53ff0ce4b9c" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t + 2,\\,y=t^{2} - 4,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(-1)+2=1, \\\\ y & =1(-1)^2-4=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e23f241-f452-5243-a0b7-f77e541a0989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558816} {:answer/id #uuid "24f40227-ba3b-508d-aeee-5e5e2370ac46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=21 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558817} {:answer/id #uuid "720482a7-dc22-5a41-8f37-f9686fbd5213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558826} {:answer/id #uuid "a50303d4-70f7-542d-b0e1-339984daa9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x - 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558831} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfa23a4-cf44-5d59-b765-9793783e11b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f40227-ba3b-508d-aeee-5e5e2370ac46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=21 - 2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558817} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b033ee4-952f-5982-801c-d387a6a5c387" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4237e233-f255-5c2f-9c7d-49a3a4554fe4" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t + 4,\\,y=t^{2} + 4,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)+4=8, \\\\ y & =1(1)^2+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(8,5).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(1)}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(5) & =-2(x-(8)) \\\\ y & =21 - 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c2729f-d1ef-5fc5-9f44-813dd842ec10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8 x - 26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558812} {:answer/id #uuid "3c242001-8b39-573b-9217-fae426b4f7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{8} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558820} {:answer/id #uuid "a80d5c91-68b9-5846-9cc9-45595191e388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6 - \\frac{x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558832} {:answer/id #uuid "baa341fe-c644-5aa2-a102-a47eb0f01112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558834} {:answer/id #uuid "c6322117-8d41-5306-a698-32ea9222530a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{8} - \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa341fe-c644-5aa2-a102-a47eb0f01112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558834} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca0585b8-289b-5b2c-9f22-18ed18a060f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc10b2ad-7055-5588-94f5-628af39c3546" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t + 3,\\,y=4 t^{2} + 2,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(1)+3=4, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2+2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(4,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{1}=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(8)}=- \\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(6) & =- \\frac{1}{8}(x-(4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ac7399-0dc1-5d89-8621-10cb21386b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558810} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf3a17-b2cc-5daa-b1d0-21b6fdfde798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1 - \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558824} {:answer/id #uuid "d22890a9-2e8e-5ea3-8f7e-88ef6576c01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558840} {:answer/id #uuid "e52b6eb7-877f-533b-b504-8437b99292eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 - \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558842} {:answer/id #uuid "f2136005-f959-5a80-8cef-7eeade6aa0ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52b6eb7-877f-533b-b504-8437b99292eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 - \\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558842} :answer-field/id #uuid "682f6100-b7ef-5a7e-aa77-624c1c84e6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558825}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf1b4822-c23a-58d8-a2b6-77ecfb6d4a0e" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t - 2,\\,y=4 t^{2} - 3,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)-2=2, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2-3=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(2)}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(1) & =- \\frac{1}{2}(x-(2)) \\\\ y & =2 - \\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2c0c5f-3772-535a-b35c-39dcc0eb618a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2c0c5f-3772-535a-b35c-39dcc0eb618a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558815} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1e5439-df26-5182-9c52-a734c31ffacb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0504c1a6-5dbf-5014-b767-efd48eeac36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0504c1a6-5dbf-5014-b767-efd48eeac36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558811} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6552b46-6d51-5ccd-946b-3dce5395b3fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558833}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2d6d05d-e6ab-5c06-8133-7210d78af47a" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t+5,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2}+3,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Give exact answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)+5=-2+5 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})+3=3+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(3,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,6)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)-(-2)) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)+2) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1+3 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =- \\frac{1}{2}(0-(3))+6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{15}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c1b256c-b58b-5262-9e7e-eb50c1e46594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} - \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558822} {:answer/id #uuid "89102a7b-bfa1-554b-9561-b35b760265db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3} - \\frac{4 x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558827} {:answer/id #uuid "a31a817d-373a-56c6-bd81-682ccb050cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558830} {:answer/id #uuid "f130c5e2-9491-5d8a-bc91-56eb5f91f664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558843} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db5f14-b4cb-57ea-9246-70de6217b324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{3 x}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31a817d-373a-56c6-bd81-682ccb050cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558830} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbcedbd-fd68-534b-be4e-3c16d18bb3fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558828}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fadeca0e-8749-557f-ac6d-8e17b0f0932b" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3 t + 1,\\,y=2 t^{2} + 2,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3(-1)+1=-2, \\\\ y & =2(-1)^2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,4).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3,\\quad y\\prime(t)=4 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{4 t}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{4(-1)}{3}=- \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a47280-bb39-5f04-b0ed-83b425f7a5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51a47280-bb39-5f04-b0ed-83b425f7a5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4d45ee-3006-5b2f-aa2c-8d00ff52e32f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49dc971-dd18-5521-952f-6a90d9772d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a49dc971-dd18-5521-952f-6a90d9772d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724870} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a210425-43cb-52f2-b47f-41ff540490a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e4c5f2c-f094-5c27-985a-4136ac4a86b1" :question/problem "![](images/29/2992cef2d7e9f4bbb9c208dca4abcd6749bf196b4fc7b609c6afa119a705a72f.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by\n\n$$\nx=t^4+t+1,\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=-1$. The dashed line is the normal at $C$. Determine the coordinates of the point where this normal crosses the $x$-axis. Use the equations to obtain exact values.\n\nThe normal crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}}, {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=-1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=(-1)^4+(-1)+1=1,\\\\\ny&=(-1)^2+1=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $C=(1,2)$. Differentiate the two coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4t^3+1,\\qquad y'(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{4t^3+1}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate it at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{4(-1)^3+1}=\\frac{-2}{-3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the normal is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{2/3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,2)$:\n\n$$\ny-2=-\\frac{3}{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute this and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2&=-\\frac{3}{2}(x-1),\\\\\nx-1&=\\frac{4}{3},\\\\\nx&=\\frac{7}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the normal crosses the $x$-axis at $\\left(\\dfrac{7}{3},0\\right)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a1a935-c6b5-5e9d-be55-613f0b75678c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724861} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0713a4-f77b-50d8-aca5-fe7d39884675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724862} {:answer/id #uuid "630f1f08-99eb-5dfb-9ecb-1734387b3f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724867} {:answer/id #uuid "d2eb1aa5-cbb6-5a1b-8d9d-c3c211345f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724872} {:answer/id #uuid "e546ba27-57f8-5448-b9ec-8af6c81792e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "630f1f08-99eb-5dfb-9ecb-1734387b3f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724867} :answer-field/id #uuid "38d7ea95-488a-51df-9ec4-f2f75d247c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a515ef87-73f9-5360-a766-e73507c149b9" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\qquad y=2t^2+1,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is given by $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2t$. Let $C$ be the point where $t=1$. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=1$ into both parametric equations to find the point:\n\n$$\nx=2(1)+1=3,\\qquad y=2(1)^2+1=3.\n$$\n\nThus $C=(3,3)$. The supplied derivative gives the tangent slope at $C$:\n\n$$\nm=2(1)=2.\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(3,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-3&=-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}+3\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58bfad5b-32b9-5924-864c-3a90dda34375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58bfad5b-32b9-5924-864c-3a90dda34375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724866} :answer-field/id #uuid "f925a96d-0cf1-5c24-b537-20d7647f90b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724875}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d83b0db7-0158-50ec-a222-919bee323c57" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\ln(t^2)+1,\\qquad y=2t^3-3t+3,\\qquad t>0.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=1$. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C$. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe normal line is $y=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First find the coordinates of $C$ by substituting $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\ln(1^2)+1=1,\\\\\ny&=2(1)^3-3(1)+3=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $C=(1,2)$. Differentiate both coordinates with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{2t}{t^2}=\\frac{2}{t},\\qquad y'(t)=6t^2-3.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of these derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t^2-3}{2/t}=\\frac{t(6t^2-3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $t=1$ this gives\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{6-3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTake its negative reciprocal to obtain the normal slope:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $(1,2)$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-2&=-\\frac{2}{3}(x-1),\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}+2,\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEnter $-\\dfrac{2}{3}x+\\dfrac{8}{3}$ in the blank after $y=$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}]}
